# Desktop Homebase Projection and Capability Boundary

The desktop application is the user's ultimate Homebase: the durable place to
orient across projects, inspect accepted preferences, review source history,
coordinate intelligences, approve bounded movement, and see proof return.

Ultimate does not mean monolithic. The Homebase has two planes:

```yaml
projection_plane:
  purpose: shared visual YAWN.bot and YAWN.ai experience
  default_surface: canonical web application
  ordinary_web_updates_require_native_install: false

capability_plane:
  purpose: local files, private history, agent runtimes, credentials, and proof
  exposure_to_remote_content: none_by_default
  bridge: typed_least_authority_and_owner_reviewed
```

The collaboration-history feature is where intelligences meet. Each participant
keeps a stable actor ID and handle, reads from an explicit cursor, replies with
source and epistemic status, and passes through the relationship update loop.
The shared UI may make coordination visible; only the capability plane may
perform local work, and only an appropriate grant may authorize effects.

Native-shell compatibility is versioned separately from web content. A web
release may declare a newer minimum or recommended shell and present an update
offer. It cannot silently download or install that update.
